<section id="processes">
<h2>The gateway owns the conversation; the broker runs providers.</h2>
<figure class="ownership-map">
<div class="map-input"><strong>Chat service ↔ gateway ↔ model endpoint</strong>
<p>Messages and replies · prompts and tool results</p>
</div>
<div class="map-zone">
<h3>Gateway · dekopond</h3>
<p>Conversation history, attachment spool and controlled shell interpreter</p>
<p><code>bash({ script })</code> dispatches builtins locally and provider commands over the socket.</p>
</div>
<p class="flow-label">Unix socket: authenticated peer + subject attestation ↓ requests / results ↑</p>
<div class="map-zone broker-zone">
<h3>Broker · dekopon-brokerd</h3>
<p>Peer/subject mapping → Cedar decision → fresh Wasm invocation</p>
<p>Native HTTP, storage and asset services enforce separate execution constraints.</p>
<p>Provider credential → native HTTP → allowed remote endpoint</p>
</div>
<figcaption>Provider credentials bypass the model and guest code. Chat-service and model credentials belong to the gateway. Both processes send telemetry to your collection backend.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>These are local processes joined by a protected Unix socket, not a multi-tenant network service. Wasmtime runs inside the broker process; a runtime vulnerability or compromised broker host defeats that boundary.</p>
</section>
